   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law and the executive law, in relation to creating a statewide central register of elder abuse and maltreatment reports and the elder justice coordinating council",
    "basePrintNo": "S8033",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8033-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 9-C §§473-m - 473-q, Soc Serv L; add Art 28 §830, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S8033\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the social services law and the executive law, in\nrelation to creating a statewide central register of elder abuse and\nmaltreatment reports and the elder justice coordinating council\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nCreates a statewide central register of elder abuse and maltreatment\nreports and the elder justice coordinating council.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the social services law to add a new\narticle 9-C to establish a statewide central register of elder abuse\nand maltreatment reports. The bill defines \"abused or maltreated\nelder\" and \"caregiver\" for purposes of the central register. Pursuant\nto the bill, the central register would be established in the office\nof children and family services and would be charged with receiving\nallegations of elder abuse or maltreatment. The bill would also\nestablish mandated reporters responsible for reporting suspected\nincidents of elder abuse or maltreatment.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends the executive law to add a new article 28\nestablishing the Elder Justice Coordinating Council. The Council would\nbe responsible for interagency planning and review of elder abuse\ninitiatives in the state. Among other things, the Council would be\nresponsible for conducting community forums to gather information\nrelating to a New York state plan for the identification,\ninvestigation, and intervention into cases of elder abuse and\nmaltreatment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnfortunately, elder abuse and maltreatment have become a pervasive\nand devastating reality for many of our nation's senior citizens.\nOver time, states have begun to respond to the increase in prevalence\nof these crimes through the establishment of state registries and\nmandated reporting procedures in suspected instances of abuse, neglect\nand exploitation. To date, New York is the only state that has not\nimplemented any professional reporting requirements. As our population\nages, and these cases continue to come to the forefront, it is time\nthat we establish a comprehensive system to address these cases in a\ntimely, responsible manner.\n\nNational experts have reported that elder abuse awareness lags behind\nthe fields of child abuse and domestic violence by as much as two\ndecades. This shortage of awareness, coupled with the fact that elder\nabuse is one of the most underreported crimes in the nation, has meant\nthat the landscape of intervention and prosecution for these crimes\nhas shown a significant lack of comprehensiveness and coordination. In\npart, the nature of elder abuse crimes exacerbates these issues.\nVictims often experience elder abuse from loved ones, friends, or\nneighbors and will refuse help due to fear, embarrassment, or the\ndesire to protect their abuser. This can lead to the continuation of\nabuse that can compromise financial, physical, and mental well-being\nand has been shown to increase mortality rates.\n\nGiven the seriousness of these crimes, and the impact that they can\nhave on the lives of seniors in New York State, it is imperative that\na comprehensive state plan for the recognition of, and intervention\ninto, cases of suspected elder abuse is established. This bill will\ncreate an Elder Justice Coordinating Council that will convene experts\nand stakeholders in important discussions of their current experiences\nregarding elder abuse cases and recommendations going forward. In\naddition, the Coordinating Council will be responsible for developing\nguidance related to the implementation of a statewide registry and\nmandated reporting system in 2018. As the last state in the nation to\ncreate a reporting system, it is vital that we ensure that the system\nwe establish draws on the lessons learned in other states, and builds\nan infrastructure that addresses the seriousness of the issues raised\nin cases of elder abuse and maltreatment in a timely and effective\nmanner.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nSubject to appropriation\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect October 1, 2016; provided that section one\nof this act shall take effect January 1, 2018.\n",
